      <tbody><tr><td>Price</td><td>$45-55</td><td>Free (built in)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Antennas</td><td>2 large external</td><td>Small internal (2x2 typical)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Max theoretical speed</td><td>1900 Mbps (600+1300)</td><td>Varies: 866-2400 Mbps typical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wi-Fi standard</td><td>Wi-Fi 5 (802.11ac)</td><td>Wi-Fi 5, 6, or 6E depending on laptop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dual-band</td><td>Yes (2.4 + 5 GHz)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Linux driver support</td><td>Chipset-dependent but well-supported</td><td>Depends on card</td></tr>
<tr><td>Better in weak-signal areas</td><td>Yes, dramatically</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Portable</td><td>Adapter + USB extension cable</td><td>Nothing to carry</td></tr></tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When ALFA AC1900 USB Wi-Fi Adapter wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick ALFA AC1900 USB Wi-Fi Adapter if...</h2>
    <p>Your laptop is in a room where Wi-Fi is genuinely bad. You see 1-2 bars, streams buffer, video calls drop. Move the router first (free); if that doesn't work, an external adapter with real antennas will pull useful signal where the internal card cannot.</p><p>You have a desktop PC without built-in Wi-Fi. USB adapters are the simplest add-Wi-Fi option — cheaper than opening the case for a PCIe card, more portable if you rearrange the room.</p><p>You need directional signal. External antennas can be aimed and repositioned; internal laptop antennas are wherever the manufacturer put them.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When Built-in Laptop Wi-Fi wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick Built-in Laptop Wi-Fi if...</h2>
    <p>Your Wi-Fi already works. If you're getting 3+ bars, streaming works, calls are stable — an external adapter is a solution to a problem you don't have.</p><p>You have a modern laptop with Wi-Fi 6 or 6E. The ALFA AC1900 is a Wi-Fi 5 (802.11ac) adapter. Adding it to a Wi-Fi 6E laptop is a downgrade for the specs.</p><p>You want zero dongles. Life is simpler when nothing dangles off the side of the laptop.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">The verdict</p>
    <p>Router placement first, always. Move the router to the center of the space, off the floor, out of a cabinet. That solves more dead-zone problems than any adapter.</p><p>If router placement can't be fixed and one specific device in one specific room needs better Wi-Fi, the ALFA is the right tool. If Wi-Fi is fine, spend the $50 on something else.</p>
  </section>

